<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mahmud Barzanji</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1878">1878</a>–<a href="/years/1956">1956</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Sheikh Mahmud Barzanji</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sheikh Mahmud Barzanji was a Kurdish leader from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sulaymaniyah">Sulaymaniyah</a> in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a> and a sheikh of the Qadiri <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sufism">Sufi</a> order. Appointed governor by the British in 1918, he soon rebelled and was exiled, but returned in 1922 and proclaimed himself king of the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kurdistan">Kurdistan</a>. British air power ended his rule by 1924, and after a last rising in 1930–31 he lived under restriction until his death in 1956.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mahmud-barzanji.jpg" alt="Mahmud Barzanji" width="308" height="500" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown authorUnknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mahmud_Barzanji.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
